{
  "concept": "For 0 < x < π/2 the circular sector of angle x in a unit circle is sandwiched between two right triangles, giving tan x / 2 ≥ x / 2 ≥ sin x / 2, which squeezes sin x / x to 1 as x → 0.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Left panel: coordinate axes with arrowheads (x-axis horizontal, y-axis vertical), origin at center",
    "Left panel: blue unit circle of radius 1 centered at origin",
    "Left panel: red radius from origin to the point (cos x, sin x) on the circle, extended as a red ray to the vertical tangent line at x = 1",
    "Left panel: red dot and label '(1, tan x)' at the top of the vertical tangent segment",
    "Left panel: red dot and label '(cos x, sin x)' on the circle with a thin arrow pointing to it",
    "Left panel: red dot and label '(1, 0)' on the x-axis",
    "Left panel: dark red horizontal base segment from origin to (1, 0)",
    "Left panel: red dashed vertical segment from (1, 0) up to (1, tan x)",
    "Left panel: red dashed chord from (cos x, sin x) down to (1, 0)",
    "Left panel: small curved arrow with label 'x' marking the angle at the origin",
    "Left panel: curly brace under the base with label '1'",
    "Right panel A: large right triangle, pale pink fill, red outline, vertical leg labeled 'tan x', base labeled '1', angle 'x' at the left vertex, blue dashed arc of the unit circle cutting through it",
    "Right panel B: circular sector of angle x, pale pink fill, red straight edges, solid blue circular arc, base labeled '1', angle 'x' at the left vertex",
    "Right panel C: small right triangle with vertices at origin, (1,0) and (cos x, sin x) scaled to same base, pale pink fill, red outline, base labeled '1', angle 'x', thin red dashed vertical drop from apex, blue dashed arc outside the triangle, curly brace on the right labeled 'sin x'",
    "Caption row: 'Area of triangle  ≥  Area of sector  ≥  Area of triangle'",
    "Formula row: 'tan x / 2  ≥  x / 2  ≥  sin x / 2'",
    "Live numeric readout of tan x / 2, x / 2, sin x / 2 and of sin x / x (added for the slider)",
    "Highlight ring / glow used when a panel label is clicked"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "angleX",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "angle x (radians)",
      "range": [0.05, 1.55, 0.01],
      "default": 0.8,
      "teaches": "That the inequality tan x / 2 ≥ x / 2 ≥ sin x / 2 holds for every angle in (0, π/2), and that as x shrinks the three areas — and hence the three quantities — converge toward each other."
    },
    {
      "id": "overlayView",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "View",
      "options": ["Side by side", "Overlaid"],
      "default": "Side by side",
      "teaches": "That the two triangles literally contain and are contained by the sector — nesting them in one picture makes the ordering of the three areas visible rather than asserted."
    },
    {
      "id": "regionFocus",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "Which region each area formula describes: clicking a label isolates that shape and shows its area expression, tying tan x / 2, x / 2, and sin x / 2 to specific geometry."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The unit-circle setup",
      "narration": "A radius at angle x meets the unit circle at (cos x, sin x), and its extension meets the vertical tangent line at (1, tan x). The base from the origin to (1, 0) has length 1.",
      "state": { "angleX": 0.8, "overlayView": "Side by side" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Three regions, three areas",
      "narration": "The same angle generates a big triangle of height tan x, a sector of angle x, and a small triangle of height sin x. Their areas are tan x / 2, x / 2, and sin x / 2.",
      "state": { "angleX": 0.8, "overlayView": "Side by side" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Nest them to see the ordering",
      "narration": "Overlaid on one picture, the small triangle sits inside the sector, which sits inside the large triangle — containment proves tan x / 2 ≥ x / 2 ≥ sin x / 2.",
      "state": { "angleX": 0.8, "overlayView": "Overlaid" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Open the angle wide",
      "narration": "Push x toward π/2 and the outer triangle explodes upward while the inner triangle barely grows: the bounds are far apart, so the inequality is loose here.",
      "state": { "angleX": 1.45, "overlayView": "Overlaid" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Squeeze toward zero",
      "narration": "As x shrinks, the three nested regions become nearly indistinguishable. Dividing the inequality by sin x / 2 gives cos x ≤ sin x / x ≤ 1 / cos x, and both bounds tend to 1.",
      "state": { "angleX": 0.1, "overlayView": "Overlaid" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Read off the limit",
      "narration": "At a tiny angle the readout shows sin x / x hugging 1, trapped between cos x and 1 / cos x — the Squeezing Theorem finishes the proof.",
      "state": { "angleX": 0.05, "overlayView": "Overlaid" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Because the sector is geometrically trapped between two triangles built from the same angle, sin x / x is trapped between cos x and 1/cos x — and both squeeze to 1 as x → 0.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Two-zone layout. Left zone: thin black axes with arrowheads, a thick sky-blue unit circle centered on the origin, red/salmon radius and tangent-ray lines, three solid red dots at (1,0), (cos x, sin x), (1, tan x), dashed red segments for the vertical tangent leg and the chord, a small curved angle arrow labeled x at the origin, and a curly brace labeled 1 under the unit base. Right zone: three shapes in a row, all sharing base length 1 and left-vertex angle x, filled pale pink with red outlines; the first is the tall right triangle (vertical leg labeled tan x) with a blue dashed arc crossing it, the second is the sector with a solid blue arc as its curved edge, the third is the short triangle with a blue dashed arc bulging just outside it and a curly brace labeled sin x on the right. Below the three shapes, two centered text rows: 'Area of triangle ≥ Area of sector ≥ Area of triangle' and beneath it the stacked fractions 'tan x / 2 ≥ x / 2 ≥ sin x / 2'. In overlaid mode, draw all three shapes concentric on a single left-vertex with graded pink opacities so the nesting reads clearly, keeping the blue arc for the sector boundary. Panel labels are clickable and should glow / desaturate the others when selected. Include a small readout box giving current x, tan x/2, x/2, sin x/2, and sin x / x. Palette: sky blue for circle/arcs, salmon-red for all straight construction lines and dots, pale pink fills, black text and axes."
}

